\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Pushforward of a reflection-positive law}
\date{2026-11-08}

\p{Let #{(\Omega,\mathcal{F})} and #{(\Omega',\mathcal{F}')} be
measurable spaces, and let #{\nu} be a probability measure on
#{(\Omega,\mathcal{F})}. Let #{\theta} and #{\theta'} be
measurable involutions of #{\Omega} and #{\Omega'}, and let
#{T:\Omega\to\Omega'} be measurable, with
#{T\circ\theta=\theta'\circ T}. Let #{A_+} be a collection of
bounded #{\mathcal{F}}-measurable complex functions such that
##{\int \overline{F(\theta\omega)}\,F(\omega)\,d\nu(\omega)\ge 0}
for every #{F\in A_+}. Let #{B_+} be a collection of bounded
#{\mathcal{F}'}-measurable complex functions #{H} with
#{H\circ T\in A_+}. Then
##{\int \overline{H(\theta'\eta)}\,H(\eta)\,d(T_*\nu)(\eta)\ge 0.}
That bounded measurable product is integrable for #{T_*\nu}.}

\p{Apply this to \ref{fdip-00FP}. Fix #{m>0} and a finite
reflection-invariant #{S} in the dyadic index, with
#{|S|\ge 2}. Let #{\nu_S} be its Gaussian law and let
#{A_+} be the bounded measurable functions of its positive
coordinates. Let #{P} be the points of #{S} strictly between
#{0} and #{1/2}, and keep #{0} and #{1/2} as coordinates
when they lie in #{S}. An empty #{P} leaves only those
coordinates. Otherwise partition #{P} into disjoint nonempty
blocks and put nonnegative weights summing to #{1} on each
block, with the same weight at the reflected site. No such
block contains a point and its image. Let #{T} send a sample
to the block averages, the reflected averages, and the kept
fixed coordinates, and let #{\theta'} swap each block with
its reflected block. Both coordinate spaces carry Borel sigma-algebras, so the
continuous maps #{T} and #{\theta'} are measurable. Take
#{B_+} to be the bounded measurable functions of the coarse
positive coordinates. The inequality holds for the law of
that coarse sample, with the normalization of
\ref{fdip-00FP}. One point in a block forces the weight
#{1} on that block. When each block has one strictly positive weight, that weight is #{1},
and #{T}, on the surviving sites, their
images, and the fixed points in #{S}, is the coordinate
projection of \ref{fdip-00FQ}. The image law is that set's
arc precision. Two positive weights in one block are not
that projection.}

\proof{
\p{Fix #{H\in B_+} and set #{F=H\circ T}, so #{F\in A_+}.
Conjugation and composition preserve measurability, so the
bounded product
##{g=\overline{H\circ\theta'}\,H}
is measurable. Preimages preserve countable disjoint unions, so
#{(T_*\nu)(A)=\nu(T^{-1}(A))} is a probability measure, and
indicators, then simple functions, satisfy
##{\int s\,d(T_*\nu)=\int (s\circ T)\,d\nu.}
Equal-subinterval left endpoints, retaining the final value,
define such an #{s} for bounded real measurable #{f}, with
#{f-s} and #{(f-s)\circ T} bounded by one subinterval length.
Simple integrals differ by at most that combined length and
approach the integral of #{f}. Real and imaginary parts extend the
identity to #{g}. Intertwining gives
##{g\circ T=\overline{F\circ\theta}\,F,}
with nonnegative source integral.}

\p{On the arc, \ref{fdip-00FP} gives the inequality for
#{A_+}. For a block #{B} with weights #{a_i},
##{(T\phi)_B=\sum_{i\in B}a_i\phi_i,\qquad
(T\phi)_{\theta B}=\sum_{i\in B}a_i\phi_{\theta i}.}
Reflection swaps the two block averages and fixes the
remaining coordinates, so #{T} intertwines the reflections.
A function in #{B_+} depends measurably on the positive
coordinates in \ref{fdip-00FP}, so its pullback lies in
#{A_+}. The substitution gives the coarse inequality.}
}
